cut:  cut is command line utility that allows your to cut parts of lines from specified files or piped data and print the result to standard output.
=== 

Note: 
1. if You might want to extract something from whitespace-separated output (ex: ls -ltr), awk is generally more appropriate
2. cut works best with a known delimiter.
3. cut is not a full CSV parser. cut doesn't understand CSV perfectly


cut -c 1 cut_test.txt           --> Extract the first character from every line.


cut -c 1,3,5 cut_test.txt       --> Extract multiple individual characters


cut -c 1-3 cut_test.txt         --> Extract a range of characters


cut -c 1-3,6-8 cut_test.txt     --> Extract multiple ranges


cut -c 5- cut_test.txt           --> Start at character 5 and continue to the end.


cut -c -5 cut_test.txt           --> Extract characters from the beginning through character 5.


cut -f 2 cut_test.txt            --> f -> To extract field 2, By default, cut -f expects TAB as the delimiter. we need -d to specify delimiter


cut -d ',' -f 2 cut_test.txt     -->  -d ','  -> Use comma as the field separator.


cut -d ',' -f 1,3 cut_test.txt   --> Extract multiple fields


cut -d ',' -f 2-4 cut_test.txt   --> Extract a range of fields


cut -d ',' -f 2- cut_test.txt    --> Extract from a field to the end


cut -d ',' -f -2 cut_test.txt    --> Extract from beginning to a field


cut -d ',' -s -f 2 cut_test.txt  --> -s means: Suppress lines that don't contain the delimiter. So only lines containing , are processed.


cut -d ',' -f 2 --complement cut_test.txt   --> You want everything except field 2.


cut -d ':' -f 1 /etc/passwd      --> it will give usernames


cut -d ':' -f 1,6 /etc/passwd    --> get usernames & home directories





